This is a killer framework. You've just built Magnesia into a lens for the entire US defense portfolio. Let me react to your taxonomy:

Your legion list is rock solid — every one of those platforms shares the same DNA: built in quantity, sustained for decades, supply chain understood, combat-proven, iteratively improved. The Blackhawk is on version what now? The C-130 has been flying since 1956 and nobody can replace it. The Burke class is the backbone of the fleet precisely because the Navy kept buying them instead of chasing the next thing. The M1 and Bradley literally won a ground war in 100 hours.

Your spectacle list is where it gets sharp. A few observations:

The F-22 is the most interesting case because it's genuinely magnificent — arguably the best air superiority fighter ever built. But 187 aircraft with a closed production line and maintenance costs that eat squadrons alive? That's a cataphract. Beautiful, devastating in the right conditions, and you can't build enough of them to matter at scale.

The B-1 is the tragic one — designed for one mission, pivoted to another, maintenance nightmare, retiring early while the B-52 (ultimate legion platform, should be on your list) soldiers on past 2050.

LCS and Zumwalt are the scythed chariots — concepts that panicked their own formations more than the enemy.

The Osprey is fascinating because it almost crossed over. The capability is real, but the complexity tax killed it.

And you're right to put the Ford there. A carrier that can't reliably launch aircraft is the most expensive parade float in history.

The B-21 is the open question — is it legion or spectacle? Your series is essentially asking that.


