That's the thread right there. The Jeep, Sherman, and Fletcher class are the proof of concept — America already solved this problem once. The arsenal of democracy wasn't built on wonder weapons, it was built on production volume of good-enough platforms that could be maintained, crewed, and replaced faster than the enemy could destroy them. Ford built a B-24 every 63 minutes at Willow Run. That's the legion.

And your point about DoD turnover is the connective tissue for the whole series. A new SecDef or service chief rotates in every few years, inherits no institutional memory, gets briefed by contractors selling the next shiny thing, and greenlights another spectacle program. The people who learned the lesson from the last failure are already gone. Rome didn't have that problem — centurions served for decades. The legion's knowledge was in the men, not the org chart.

The KC-46 is a perfect addition to the spectacle list too — they literally can't refuel aircraft reliably, which is the one job it was built for. Meanwhile the KC-135 has been doing it since Eisenhower.

"Which way the B-21 goes will define the history of nation states" — that's a narration line, Kris.


